BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gainssa-ai-safety-essentials-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261108
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261109
SUMMARY:Deadline: GAIN SSA: AI Safety Essentials Fellowship (inaugural coho
 rt)
DESCRIPTION:GAIN SSA (Global AI Youth Network\, Sub-Saharan Africa) runs th
 e AI Safety Essentials Fellowship for residents of Africa considering how 
 they can be effective contributors in shaping the continent's transition t
 oward containing risks from transformative AI. Fellows spend six weeks map
 ping the priorities and gaps that need to be filled in Africa's AI safety 
 ecosystem\, through curated pre-reading and a live group call each week (a
 bout 5 hours per week). CURRICULUM: existential risks and catastrophic vec
 tors\; frontier AI models and Africa\; defining safe and beneficial AI\; s
 ubfields and alignment architectures\; Africa's AI safety landscape and po
 licy\; and a capstone week on fellowships and career launch\, with guidanc
 e toward international pipelines such as MATS\, ERA\, BlueDot and GovAI. M
 entorship comes from international AI safety researchers\, governance fell
 ows and peer builders. FUNDING: after the program\, top capstone action-pl
 an proposals are funded from a USD 500 to 1\,000 micro-grant pool (capped 
 at USD 1\,000)\, including proposals needing compute\, travel to AI alignm
 ent conferences in Sub-Saharan Africa\, or communication work (for example
  Substack Pro for writers)\; results are shared within 10 days of receivin
 g a proposal. The main aim is to have participants take action to build Af
 rica's first AI safety systems. APPLICATION: expression of interest for th
 e inaugural cohort via the Google Form linked from the program page. DEADL
 INE: expressions of interest close 8 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: GAIN 
 SSA (Global AI Youth Network\, Sub-Saharan Africa)\nLocation: Online (virt
 ual\, facilitated). Open to residents of African countries.\nAward: Micro-
 grant pool funding top capstone proposals at USD 500 to 1\,000 each (cappe
 d at USD 1\,000)\, usable for compute access\, communication work such as 
 Substack Pro subscriptions for writers\, or travel to AI alignment confere
 nces in Sub-Saharan Africa. The fellowship itself is free to attend (no pa
 rticipation fee\, no application fee)\; no stipend is paid during the prog
 ram.\nDuration: 6-week facilitated virtual curriculum plus an onboarding p
 re-requisites module (the site also describes it as 7 weeks)\, about 5 hou
 rs per week with one live group call per week\; capstone proposals are fun
 ded afterwards\, with results within 10 days of submission.\n\nMore: https
 ://gainssa.org/initiatives/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://gainssa.org/initiatives/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online (virtual\, facilitated). Open to residents of African count
 ries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Fellowship 2027-2028 for Artists\, Scholars and T
 hinkers (Cassis\, France)
DESCRIPTION:The Camargo Foundation invites artists\, scholars and thinkers 
 to its flagship Core Fellowship: a ten-week self-directed residency on its
  clifftop campus overlooking the Mediterranean in Cassis\, France\, dedica
 ted to research\, experimentation and interdisciplinary exchange. Curators
  apply under the Thinkers category\, which covers accomplished professiona
 ls and practitioners in cultural and creative fields who are professionall
 y engaged in critical thought\, including curators\, critics and journalis
 ts\; projects should engage theoretical\, artistic or societal questions. 
 WHAT YOU GET: a stipend of EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 total)\, basic tra
 nsportation funding\, and a furnished apartment with an equipped kitchen o
 n campus. Fourteen fellowships are awarded per year: seven for artists and
  seven for scholars and thinkers. RESIDENCY PERIODS: Fall 2027 (8 Septembe
 r to 17 November 2027) or Spring 2028 (23 February to 4 May 2028). ELIGIBI
 LITY: artists in visual arts\, performance\, writing\, film\, music or mul
 tidisciplinary work with at least 5 years of professional practice\; schol
 ars holding or pursuing a PhD focused on French and Francophone cultures o
 r cross-cultural Mediterranean studies\; and thinkers as described above. 
 APPLICATION: via Submittable with a 100-word proposal summary\, a 1\,200-w
 ord proposal narrative\, a 300-word rationale for Camargo\, a 300-word car
 eer-stage rationale\, a CV (max 3 pages)\, work samples and two references
 . NOTE: the foundation has announced that this is the final Fellowship sel
 ected through a public open call until further notice\; a new selection pr
 ocess begins in July 2027\, making this the last chance to apply directly.
 \n\nOrganisation: Camargo Foundation\nLocation: Camargo Foundation campus\
 , Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.\nAward: EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 to
 tal for the ten-week residency)\, plus basic travel funding and a furnishe
 d apartment with equipped kitchen on the Camargo campus. 14 fellowships pe
 r year: 7 for artists and 7 for scholars and thinkers. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Ten weeks\, either 8 September to 17 November 2027 or 23 Febru
 ary to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fello
 wship-2026-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Camargo Foundation campus\, Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:culture360-asef-arts-in-the-wake-of-ai-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260929
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260930
SUMMARY:Deadline: culture360 / ASEF: Call for Content\, Arts in the Wake of
  AI
DESCRIPTION:culture360.ASEF.org\, the arts platform of the Asia-Europe Foun
 dation\, invites proposals from writers and content producers for its open
  call 'Arts in the Wake of AI': articles\, podcasts and video interviews t
 hat foreground how cultural professionals and organisations across Asia an
 d Europe are adapting to AI technologies\, from generative tools and machi
 ne learning to recommender systems and algorithms. WHAT THEY WANT: case-st
 udy-based contributions grounded in real-world evidence\, experiences or p
 ractices\, on topics including the impact of AI on cultural diversity\, fa
 irness and inclusivity\; the environmental costs of AI in the cultural sec
 tor\; and AI governance in the cultural sector (policy\, copyright\, intel
 lectual property and the ethical use of AI-generated cultural content). Ac
 ademic research papers and non-arts subjects are not considered. FEE: SGD 
 250 per commissioned article\, podcast or video interview. FORMATS: journa
 listic articles of 800 to 1\,000 words with at least four credited images\
 ; podcasts and video interviews of 15 to 20 minutes. ELIGIBILITY: national
 s of ASEM partner countries with previous experience in writing\, podcasti
 ng or video production\, proficiency in English\, and knowledge of or invo
 lvement in the Asian and European arts and culture scene\; an existing cha
 nnel for further dissemination is an advantage\; the work is fully virtual
 . Up to two proposals per application. Only selected writers are notified.
  Queries to culture[at]asef.org. Deadline 29 September 2026 at 6 PM Singap
 ore time\, via the online application form.\n\nOrganisation: Asia-Europe F
 oundation (ASEF)\, culture360.ASEF.org\nLocation: Fully remote\; contribut
 ors do not need to be based in Singapore. Open to nationals of ASEM partne
 r countries across Asia and Europe.\nAward: SGD 250 fee per commissioned a
 rticle\, podcast or video interview. Up to two proposals per application\,
  of which at most one is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: One-off 
 editorial commissions: articles of 800 to 1\,000 words with at least four 
 credited pictures\, podcasts and video interviews of 15 to 20 minutes\n\nM
 ore: https://culture360.asef.org/opportunities/call-for-content-arts-ai/?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culture360.asef.org/opportunities/call-for-content-arts-ai/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully remote\; contributors do not need to be based in Singapore. 
 Open to nationals of ASEM partner countries across Asia and Europe.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iaps-ai-policy-fellowship-spring-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260927
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260928
SUMMARY:Deadline: IAPS: AI Policy Fellowship Spring 2027 (DC\, London or re
 mote)
DESCRIPTION:The Institute for AI Policy and Strategy (IAPS) runs a three-mo
 nth\, fully funded AI policy fellowship placing fellows on concrete AI gov
 ernance and policy research projects. Spring 2027 cohort: 22 February to 1
 4 May 2027\, opening with a mandatory in-person component in Washington DC
  from 22 February to 5 March 2027\, after which fellows work from the DC o
 ffice\, the London office or remotely. STIPENDS: USD 18\,000 for Fellows a
 nd USD 24\,000 for Senior Fellows\, plus USD 3\,000 extra on the in-person
  DC or London tracks\, healthcare benefits and a USD 500 professional deve
 lopment stipend. ELIGIBILITY: no technical background or prior policy expe
 rience required\; the DC track requires the right to work in the US betwee
 n 22 February and 14 May 2027\, and no visa sponsorship is available for t
 he US or UK\; the remote track is open globally with country-specific exce
 ptions. TIMELINE: initial responses mid-October 2026\, live interviews in 
 November\, final selections early December. Deadline 27 September 2026. Ap
 ply at https://www.guidedtrack.com/programs/ll4vpg7/run.\n\nOrganisation: 
 Institute for AI Policy and Strategy (IAPS)\nLocation: Washington DC or Lo
 ndon track (desk space provided)\, or fully remote from most countries. Ma
 ndatory in-person opening in Washington DC\, 22 February to 5 March 2027. 
 No visa sponsorship for the US or UK.\nAward: Fellows receive USD 18\,000 
 and Senior Fellows USD 24\,000 as a stipend for the three-month full-time 
 program\, plus an additional USD 3\,000 stipend on the in-person DC or Lon
 don tracks\, healthcare benefits and a USD 500 professional development st
 ipend. Up to 45 fellows. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months full-
 time\, 22 February to 14 May 2027\; extensions of 4 to 12 weeks were offer
 ed to more than half of the previous cohort\n\nMore: https://www.iaps.ai/f
 ellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iaps.ai/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC or London track (desk space provided)\, or fully rem
 ote from most countries. Mandatory in-person opening in Washington DC\, 22
  February to 5 March 2027. No visa sponsorship for the US or UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucla-meap-modern-endangered-archives-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261117
SUMMARY:Deadline: UCLA Modern Endangered Archives Program (MEAP): 2026-27 C
 all for Applications
DESCRIPTION:The UCLA Library Modern Endangered Archives Program (MEAP)\, fu
 nded by Arcadia\, grants its ninth cohort of awards to preserve\, document
  and digitize cultural heritage collections at risk from environmental con
 ditions\, political uncertainty\, inherently unsustainable media\, inappro
 priate storage\, or communal and social change. IN SCOPE: 20th and 21st ce
 ntury materials\, including print\, photographic\, film\, video\, audio\, 
 ephemeral and born-digital objects\; all funded materials are published op
 en access in the UCLA Library Digital Collections repository. GRANT TYPES:
  Planning Grants up to USD 20\,000 over one year for organization\, survey
  and inventory work before digitization\; Project Grants up to USD 70\,000
  over two years for digitization and metadata creation on collections read
 y to digitize\, or for curating existing digital assets\; Regional Grants 
 up to USD 100\,000 over two years for digital collections combining materi
 als from three or more institutions\, families or archival repositories ar
 ound one theme\, community or historical event\, hosted by an organizing i
 nstitution with prior MEAP grant experience (consult MEAP staff before app
 lying). There is also an invitation-only Emergency Grant track up to USD 1
 0\,000 (email meap@library.ucla.edu)\, and a new AV Grants track up to USD
  100\,000 for audiovisual archives opening April 2027 with selections in S
 eptember 2027. TIMELINE: applications opened 14 September 2026\; prelimina
 ry application webinar 15 October 2026\; preliminary applications due Mond
 ay 16 November 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific Time via meap.smapply.io\; prelimin
 ary decisions December 2026\; detailed applications due 12 February 2027\;
  awards announced summer 2027. Applications name teams working with the in
 stitutions\, families or repositories that hold the collections\; read the
  Program Guidelines and Project Eligibility documents on the MEAP site bef
 ore applying.\n\nOrganisation: UCLA Library\, Modern Endangered Archives P
 rogram\nLocation: Worldwide. Projects digitize at-risk collections where t
 hey are held\; digitized materials are published open access in the UCLA L
 ibrary Digital Collections.\nAward: Planning Grants up to USD 20\,000 (up 
 to 1 year) for collection evaluation\, survey and inventory work before di
 gitization. Project Grants up to USD 70\,000 (up to 2 years) for digitizat
 ion and metadata creation on well-organized collections\, or curation of e
 xisting digital assets. Regional Grants up to USD 100\,000 (up to 2 years)
  for digital collections drawing on three or more institutions\, families 
 or repositories around one theme\, hosted by an organization with prior ME
 AP experience. A separate AV Grants track (up to USD 100\,000\, for audio\
 , video and film) opens in April 2027. No application fee.\nDuration: Plan
 ning Grants up to 1 year\; Project and Regional Grants up to 2 years\n\nMo
 re: https://meap.library.ucla.edu/call-for-applications/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://meap.library.ucla.edu/call-for-applications/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide. Projects digitize at-risk collections where they are he
 ld\; digitized materials are published open access in the UCLA Library Dig
 ital Collections.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech-policy-press-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:Tech Policy Press is recruiting its 2027 fellowship cohort to p
 ursue independent reporting and analysis on urgent questions at the inters
 ection of technology and democracy. Since 2023 the programme has supported
  25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts\, writing on AI governa
 nce\, tech regulation\, digital rights and the entanglement of state and c
 orporate power. WHO IT IS FOR: mid-career journalists\, researchers\, lawy
 ers and public policy professionals with a strong background in a specific
  tech policy area\, a particular geography\, or technology's impact on dem
 ocracy and society. Applicants from the Global Majority and from communiti
 es underrepresented in media are encouraged\, as are projects that centre 
 communities and regions usually excluded from global tech policy conversat
 ions. Contributions can range from original reporting to policy analysis\,
  and technical practitioners are welcome as long as the work fits the publ
 ication's editorial purview. EXPECTATIONS: at least one piece of analysis 
 or reporting per month (exclusive to Tech Policy Press\, though it may be 
 republished elsewhere)\, a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\, a 
 monthly check-in with the editorial team\, collaboration with other fellow
 s and the masthead\, agreed deadlines\, and disclosure of conflicts of int
 erest. Applicants should arrive with a clear area of focus and a sense of 
 how the work will inform both the public and decision-makers. APPLY via th
 e online form by 11:59pm EDT on 24 September 2026\, with a CV or resume\, 
 a cover letter of no more than two pages\, links to prior publications (op
 en access\, published within the past three years\, at least one in Englis
 h and at least one solo-authored\; social posts\, pre-prints and unpublish
 ed coursework do not count) and two professional references. Shortlisted c
 andidates may be invited to an online interview. Decisions are expected by
  early December 2026. Two optional Zoom information sessions run on 9 Sept
 ember at 8am EDT and 16 September at 7pm EDT\; registration required.\n\nO
 rganisation: Tech Policy Press\nLocation: Fully remote\, worldwide. Intern
 ational applicants explicitly encouraged\; fellows work as independent con
 tractors. No requirement to be authorised to work in the USA.\nAward: USD 
 10\,000 stipend per fellow\, paid quarterly. Also included: editorial guid
 ance from the Tech Policy Press team\, membership of an international coho
 rt with regular feedback and exchange\, and monthly sessions to strengthen
  the work. Fellows' work is published on the Tech Policy Press website and
  promoted through the newsletter\, podcast and events. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Twelve months\, part-time\, running January to December 2027\n
 \nMore: https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-polic
 y-press-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-pre
 ss-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Fully remote\, worldwide. International applicants explicitly enco
 uraged\; fellows work as independent contractors. No requirement to be aut
 horised to work in the USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:inato-gallery-touch-some-grass-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Inato Gallery: Touch Some Grass (open-call poster exhibit
 ion)
DESCRIPTION:Touch Some Grass is an open-call poster exhibition responding t
 o the growing presence of AI-generated imagery and its impact on creative 
 labour\, authorship and visual culture. At a time when images can be produ
 ced instantly and consumed just as quickly\, the exhibition reclaims the p
 oster as a space for ideas\, intention and public conversation\, inviting 
 artists to make work that questions\, challenges or reflects on this chang
 ing landscape. As the organisers put it\, the question is no longer whethe
 r machines can make images\, they can\; the question is what kind of world
  we build when speed matters more than thought. NOTE: this call offers exh
 ibition and visibility only\, with no artist fee or stipend. SUBMISSION GU
 IDELINES: artwork size A3 (297 x 420 mm)\, portrait orientation\, 300 DPI\
 , CMYK recommended\, print-quality PDF preferred\, maximum file size 1 GB\
 , one submission per artist. CAP: participation is limited to 60 artists a
 nd the submission form closes automatically once all slots are filled\, so
  the effective deadline may fall well before 31 August 2026 (10 of 60 slot
 s were taken at the time of listing). Inato Gallery is an artist-run space
  in Iligan City\, Philippines\, which began as a solo street exhibition by
  KaraTula and now runs concept-driven solo and group shows\, open mics\, t
 alks and workshops built on openness\, participation and the belief that a
 rt should exist where people already are.\n\nOrganisation: Inato Gallery\n
 Location: Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or tr
 avel is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-
 run space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stat
 ed.\nAward: No artist fee\, stipend or production budget. This is an exhib
 ition opportunity: selected posters are shown in Inato Gallery's Touch Som
 e Grass exhibition. No submission fee is stated in the call.\nDuration: On
 e-off poster exhibition. Exhibition dates are not announced yet.\n\nMore: 
 https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or trav
 el is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-ru
 n space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stated
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ars-biologica-artxscience-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ars Biologica ArtXScience Residency 2027-2028 (Ars Electr
 onica x Ceske Budejovice ECoC 2028)
DESCRIPTION:A fully funded art-science residency from Ars Electronica and C
 eske Budejovice\, European Capital of Culture 2028\, exploring how art and
  science can collaborate on urgent environmental challenges: climate emerg
 ence\, sustainable agriculture\, geoengineering\, permaculture\, water res
 ource management\, and the broader ecological impacts on local communities
 . Selected residents work in direct collaboration with local scientists in
  Ceske Budejovice. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, designers\, creatives and arti
 st collectives with more than 5 years of professional creative practice an
 d documented interdisciplinary work\; experience in community engagement a
 nd science collaborations is essential\; applicants must be 18+\, of any n
 ationality\, and legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. WHAT YOU G
 ET: a EUR 24\,000 package (EUR 6\,000 artist fee\, up to EUR 15\,000 produ
 ction\, up to EUR 3\,000 travel and subsistence) plus one month of housing
  covered separately\; two winners are selected. STRUCTURE: the residency r
 uns January 2027 to April 2028\, with a required on-site stay of up to one
  month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/September 2027\
 , and a March 2028 stay for festival installation\, exhibition and deinsta
 llation. HOW TO APPLY: online form with project title\, a written proposal
  of max 250 words\, a scientific direction statement (max 100 words)\, art
 istic vision (max 100 words)\, community connection plan (max 100 words)\,
  a super short pitch (3 to 30 words)\, 3 to 7 images\, an optional 1-minut
 e video\, a portfolio link and contact information. Two-round selection (o
 nline application then interview)\; winners notified by November 2026. Dea
 dline 28 September 2026 at midnight CET.\n\nOrganisation: Ars Electronica 
 and Ceske Budejovice - European Capital of Culture 2028\nLocation: Ceske B
 udejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any nationality aged 18+
  who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Required on-site s
 tays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/
 September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibition and deinstal
 lation.\nAward: EUR 24\,000 total package per winner: EUR 6\,000 artist fe
 e\, up to EUR 15\,000 production budget\, and up to EUR 3\,000 for travel\
 , accommodation and subsistence\, plus a separate budget covering one mont
 h of housing in Ceske Budejovice. Two positions available. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: January 2027 to April 2028\, with on-site blocks in early 2027 
 and March 2028\n\nMore: https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars
 -biologica-art-science-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars-biologica-art-scienc
 e-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ceske Budejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any natio
 nality aged 18+ who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Req
 uired on-site stays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional 
 stay in August/September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibiti
 on and deinstallation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:knowcase-sonar-cotec-open-call-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: KNOWCASE Open Call 2027: permanent digital-culture instal
 lation in Madrid (Sonar+D x Fundacion Cotec)
DESCRIPTION:Fundacion Cotec (a Spanish innovation-focused non-profit) and S
 onar+D\, building on an initial experiment at Sonar 2026\, invite proposal
 s for a long-term installation at KNOWCASE\, the street-level showcase of 
 Cotec's Madrid headquarters in Chamberi\, visible to the public 24 hours a
  day. THEME: digital culture\, meaning projects that combine knowledge\, r
 esearch\, science and data with art\, technology and social impact. Cotec'
 s stated priorities are the big transitions (analogue to digital\, linear 
 to circular economy\, tangible to intangible) plus innovation ecosystems a
 nd R&D&I\, the climate crisis\, the future of work and learning\, social d
 ivides\, citizen participation and democratic health. WHAT YOU GET: a budg
 et of up to EUR 100\,000 plus VAT covering everything (design\, production
 \, installation\, maintenance\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, acc
 ommodation and insurance)\, and a minimum 24-month public display from Feb
 ruary 2027. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, collectives\, designers\, technologis
 ts\, universities\, research centres\, startups\, businesses\, creative ag
 encies\, producers\, cultural institutions and multidisciplinary teams\, i
 nternationally\; applicants must demonstrate solid experience and a releva
 nt portfolio. CONSTRAINTS: the proposal must be original and never previou
 sly exhibited\, must include participative or interactive elements\, must 
 encourage critical thinking\, and must be technically and financially viab
 le for the full 24+ month exhibition period (durability and maintenance ar
 e central selection criteria). Selection weighs conceptual quality highest
  (40 percent)\, followed by public interactivity (25 percent). HOW TO APPL
 Y: one PDF of maximum 15 pages containing summary\, concept\, technical pr
 oposal\, visuals\, budget\, timeline\, team profile\, portfolio and declar
 ation\, in Spanish\, another official language of Spain\, or English\, via
  the application portal linked from the terms and conditions. Results anno
 unced 14 October 2026. Deadline 7 September 2026 at midnight CEST.\n\nOrga
 nisation: Fundacion Cotec and Sonar+D\nLocation: Installation at the KNOWC
 ASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headquarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Sp
 ain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. The call is international\; appli
 cations accepted in Spanish\, other official languages of Spain\, or Engli
 sh.\nAward: Up to EUR 100\,000 (plus VAT)\, all-inclusive: conceptualisati
 on\, design\, technical development\, production\, installation\, maintena
 nce\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, accommodation and insurance.\
 nDuration: Installation displayed for a minimum of 24 months\, starting Fe
 bruary 2027\n\nMore: https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Installation at the KNOWCASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headq
 uarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Spain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. 
 The call is international\; applications accepted in Spanish\, other offic
 ial languages of Spain\, or English.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aixdesign-grain-cookbook-critical-ai-literacy-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: AIxDESIGN: Grain\, Open Call for Critical AI Literacy Rec
 ipes
DESCRIPTION:Grain is a cookbook of critical and creative AI literacy exerci
 ses for educators in film and the wider audiovisual and creative field\, c
 o-produced by AIxDESIGN and the Netherlands Film Academy and led by Nadia 
 Piet. It publishes in March/April 2027\, digital and print\, under CC BY-S
 A 4.0. The current open call is to propose a recipe by the end of August 2
 026 and join as a launch contributor. WHAT THEY WANT: tried-and-tested gro
 up exercises you have actually run with people in teaching or workshops\, 
 specifically in the context of film\, audiovisual work\, time-based media 
 and storytelling. Hands-on or analog\, 15 minutes or a full day\, beginner
  or advanced\; the one hard requirement is that another educator can read 
 your recipe and run it themselves. What that might look like: hands-on exp
 eriments with specific AI tools (image generators\, chatbots\, code assist
 ants\, video models)\; analog activities that reveal computational logic t
 hrough embodied practice\; critical readings\, watchings or listenings wit
 h structured discussion\; provocations\, games or role-plays that get at h
 ow AI sees\, thinks or makes\; and exercises that help people work out how
  they want to relate to AI in their own practice. WHAT MAKES A GOOD RECIPE
 : it helps a room of people think together and trusts the room to be smart
  given the right container\; it emerges from real practice rather than an 
 untested idea\; it is specific about what participants do\, step by step\,
  so a stranger could pick it up and adapt it to their group\, time\, tools
  or context\; it has at least one designed social moment where the thinkin
 g happens between people rather than privately\; it is about something lar
 ger than technique (how a machine sees an image\, where your values bump u
 p against what the tools want from you\, what a film's rhythm looks like l
 aid out in space instead of time)\; it takes a position without telling pe
 ople what to think\; and it points beyond itself with references\, reading
  and reference projects. Published example recipes include Image Forgery: 
 Archival Mimesis with AI (Artefacto)\, Machine Ways of Seeing (Nadia Piet)
 \, AI Film Now: A Close Watching Exercise (Nadia Piet) and Outside the Tim
 eline: Spatial Grid + Visual Clustering (Artefacto)\, alongside a root rec
 ipe template. WHAT LAUNCH CONTRIBUTORS GET: your recipe in the first editi
 on\, print and online\, credited to you\; a print copy when it lands and a
 n invite to the launch event in Amsterdam in spring 2027\; support turning
  your exercise into a recipe\, via Recipe Press (their LLM helper)\, the c
 ook-along\, or directly with the team\; and a need-based fee of EUR 150 pe
 r recipe for freelance and independent practitioners\, which you request i
 n the proposal form. After this round contributions stay open but web-only
 : no fee\, no dedicated support\, no print. COOK-ALONG: one live 30-minute
  online session on Wednesday 12 August 2026 at 16:00 CEST\, where you brin
 g a rough exercise and turn it into a recipe on the spot with Recipe Press
  (sign up at luma.com/84w148in). HOW IT WORKS: (1) propose your recipe thr
 ough the proposal form (who you are\, what the exercise is\, where you hav
 e run it\, and whether you are requesting the fee)\; a short pitch is enou
 gh\, and if it is already written you can just link your draft\; (2) they 
 reply on a rolling basis\, with one reply covering both whether it is a ma
 tch for the first edition and the fee if you requested it\, so the earlier
  you send the earlier you hear back\; (3) once it is a yes\, write it up u
 sing the structure on the Contribute a Recipe page\, with Recipe Press or 
 at the cook-along. TIMELINE: proposals open until the end of August 2026\,
  reviewed as they come in\; accepted recipes due in full by mid-September 
 2026\; Grain publishes March/April 2027. Questions to hello@aixdesign.co.\
 n\nOrganisation: AIxDESIGN\, co-produced with the Netherlands Film Academy
 \nLocation: Remote/online\; open to educators and practitioners based anyw
 here\nAward: Need-based fee of EUR 150 per recipe for freelance and indepe
 ndent practitioners: say so in the proposal form if making the time would 
 be hard without it\, and it is settled in the same reply. Launch contribut
 ors also get their recipe in the first edition of Grain in print and onlin
 e\, credited to them\, a print copy when it lands\, an invite to the launc
 h event in Amsterdam in spring 2027\, and support turning the exercise int
 o a recipe. Published under CC BY-SA 4.0\; you keep authorship. No applica
 tion fee.\nDuration: One-off contribution: proposals by end of August 2026
 \, accepted recipes due in full by mid-September 2026\; Grain publishes Ma
 rch/April 2027\n\nMore: https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-Grain-Cook
 book-for-Critical-AI-Literacy-38e37d003833804380acd027b6041d5a?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-Grain-Cookbook-for-Critical-AI-
 Literacy-38e37d003833804380acd027b6041d5a?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote/online\; open to educators and practitioners based anywhere
END:VEVENT
