UID:wilson-center-research-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Wilson Center Research Fellowship\, 2027-2028 Class
DESCRIPTION:The Wilson Center\, the congressionally chartered nonpartisan p
 olicy research institution in Washington DC\, awards 10 to 20 research fel
 lowships for its 2027-2028 class. Fellows conduct research and write in th
 eir areas of interest while interacting with policymakers in Washington an
 d with other fellows. The Center accepts policy-relevant\, nonpartisan pro
 posals that address key foreign policy challenges confronting the United S
 tates and the world\, and projects must align with at least one of the Cen
 ter's four scholarship pillars: strategic competition\, economic statecraf
 t\, TECHNOLOGY AND INNOVATION\, and regional scholarship. The technology a
 nd innovation pillar is the relevant route for AI governance\, emerging te
 chnology and digital policy projects\, which the Center also supports thro
 ugh its Science and Technology Innovation Program. Fellows work remotely f
 or the duration of the fellowship\; the Center covers travel costs for two
  required trips to Washington DC at the beginning and the conclusion of th
 e term. The stipend is USD 10\,000 per month for the twelve-month term\, w
 hich runs 1 June 2027 to 30 May 2028. ELIGIBILITY: academic candidates nee
 d a PhD in hand and either a published book or monograph beyond the disser
 tation\, or a substantial body of research articles (five or more) in repu
 table peer-reviewed journals. Non-academic applicants (practitioners\, jou
 rnalists\, public intellectuals) need at least ten years of relevant exper
 ience and a record of substantial policy impact. There is no citizenship r
 estriction\, but English proficiency is required. Applications\, including
  letters of recommendation\, must be submitted online via Interfolio by 11
 :59pm ET on 30 September 2026. Questions: fellowships@wilsoncenter.org. NO
 TE: this is an open-topic foreign policy fellowship\, not an AI-specific o
 ne\; the technology angle depends on how you scope the proposal.\n\nOrgani
 sation: Woodrow Wilson International Center for Scholars\nLocation: Remote
  for the duration of the fellowship\, with two required trips to Washingto
 n DC (at the start and the end) for which travel costs are covered.\nAward
 : USD 10\,000 per month for a twelve-month fellowship\, plus covered trave
 l for the two required trips to Washington DC. No application fee.\nDurati
 on: Twelve months\, 1 June 2027 to 30 May 2028\n\nMore: https://www.wilson
 center.org/research-fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.wilsoncenter.org/research-fellowship?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote for the duration of the fellowship\, with two required trip
 s to Washington DC (at the start and the end) for which travel costs are c
 overed.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:impart-nalli-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Impart & Nalli Fellowships 2027-28
DESCRIPTION:A research fellowship from Impart\, an online platform building
  accessible resources on South Asia's art histories\, in collaboration wit
 h Nalli Silks\, a heritage textile brand established in 1928. The programm
 e has supported two research projects a year since 2024-25\; for the 2027-
 28 cycle two fellowships are awarded\, each carrying a grant of up to INR 
 5.5 lakhs for one year of independent research and project development. AR
 EAS OF FOCUS: underexplored textile-related histories\, practices and trad
 itions across South Asia. Any textile-centric subject may be proposed\; th
 e call particularly encourages textiles in religious\, literary\, liturgic
 al and mythological contexts\; the environmental impact of textile product
 ion and consumption\; endangered or disappearing textile traditions\; crit
 ical perspectives on textile revivalism\, digitisation\, archives and mate
 rial afterlives\; labour in textile production with attention to caste\, c
 lass and gender\; relationships between textiles and other cultural forms 
 including storytelling\, poetry\, music\, architecture and material cultur
 e\; techniques\, technologies and tools tied to specific traditions\; Sout
 h Asian textiles within the contemporary global design economy\; intellect
 ual property\, ownership and cultural rights\; and socio-cultural perspect
 ives on the relationship between handmade and industrial textiles. ELIGIBI
 LITY: open to anyone aged 18 or over residing anywhere in the world\, prov
 ided the project engages with textile practices in the wider South Asia re
 gion (Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\,
  Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka\, Tibet). Early- and mid-career researchers\
 , designers\, archivists\, journalists\, writers\, educators\, practitione
 rs\, collectives and small organisations are all welcome\, and collaborati
 ve projects are permitted provided co-creators are credited and listed in 
 the application. Both new and ongoing projects are eligible\, but applican
 ts must specify which part of the project the grant will develop and discl
 ose any current or previous funding from other organisations. SUPPORTED FO
 RMATS: journalistic inquiry\, documentary film\, archive activation\, prac
 tice-based research\, archive generation\, digitisation and documentation 
 initiatives\, and exhibition or book projects. DELIVERABLES: a comprehensi
 ve project report at the end of the grant period\; a research dossier deve
 loped from that report in collaboration with Impart\, presenting key quest
 ions and findings and hosted on Impart's website for public benefit\; and 
 a set of final project outputs\, which may be audio-visual (interviews\, s
 hort films)\, visual essays (photographic or process-based documentation)\
 , written pieces (essays\, features\, interviews) or curatorial work (exhi
 bition or digital curation). Fellows also take part in periodic check-ins 
 and submit progress reports. Copyright is settled case by case\, but all f
 inal outputs must be made openly and freely accessible to the public. APPL
 ICATION: a Google Form structured as a full project proposal\, plus a proj
 ect budget (percentage-based allocation is acceptable\, but be as specific
  as possible)\, a project timeline for the one-year period with key phases
 \, activities and milestones\, and a single supporting PDF containing an u
 p-to-date CV\, selected work samples\, and up to two letters of support or
  recommendation. The form is in English\; applicants who would prefer anot
 her language are invited to write to Impart to arrange an alternative. NOT
 E: the fellowship has a strict no-tolerance policy on the use of AI or LLM
 s in the application form or any submitted materials\, and any violation r
 esults in rejection. KEY DATES: applications close 31 August 2026\; awarde
 es notified December 2026\; fellowships run January to December 2027. No i
 ndividual feedback is given on unsuccessful applications.\n\nOrganisation:
  Impart in collaboration with Nalli Silks\nLocation: Remote and self-direc
 ted\; applicants may reside anywhere in the world. Projects must engage wi
 th textile practices across South Asia: Afghanistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\
 , India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pakistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
 \nAward: Up to INR 5.5 lakhs (INR 550\,000) per fellow towards project exe
 cution\, covering research-related expenses including travel\, fieldwork a
 nd documentation subject to approval. Two fellowships are awarded. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One year\, January to December 2027\n\nMore: https
 ://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://imp-art.org/fellowship-grant/impart-x-nalli-fellowships/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote and self-directed\; applicants may reside anywhere in the w
 orld. Projects must engage with textile practices across South Asia: Afgha
 nistan\, Bangladesh\, Bhutan\, India\, the Maldives\, Myanmar\, Nepal\, Pa
 kistan\, Sri Lanka and Tibet.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:normsy-social-media-measurement-methods-incubator-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Normsy: Social Media Measurement Methods Incubator RFP
DESCRIPTION:Normsy\, a project of Civic Health Project\, runs a small metho
 ds incubator for new strategies to measure or estimate the impact of proso
 cial social-media interventions. Normsy identifies toxic posts (typically 
 content attacking or violating civic norms) and offers real users a set of
  AI-generated prosocial comments to choose from and post\; the open questi
 on is how to estimate the effect of those comments on content producers\, 
 other commenters and bystanders who consume content without interacting me
 asurably. The current approach combines survey experiments (how active X u
 sers respond emotionally to threads with and without the intervention\, an
 d how it shifts agreement with and support for the original post) with pai
 d X API platform data (classifying comments before and after an interventi
 on\, plus coarse engagement metrics that cost money each time they are rec
 ollected). WHAT THEY WANT: a systematic way to leverage available platform
  data and maximise the utility of the survey experiments. Example proposal
 s include a strategy for measuring toxicity/prosociality and reach/visibil
 ity of a thread over time with a randomisation design generating a control
  group of threads\, or an approach to benchmarking the prosocial potential
  of AI-generated comments through an iterative arena with human judges or 
 a theory-backed LLM validation approach with human validation. The brief i
 s explicit that methodological innovation\, causal identification\, scalab
 le measurement frameworks and novel observational approaches matter far mo
 re than proposing new intervention types\; proposals need not study Normsy
  exclusively but must explain how the evaluation design transfers to it. E
 thics and IRB considerations are central and Normsy will collaborate with 
 selected researchers on ethics review and implementation planning. Researc
 hers retain publication independence and IP\; the partnership is expected 
 to produce data for an academic paper. SUBMIT: one zipped folder through t
 he linked Google Form containing a 3-page proposal (design\, work already 
 done\, work still needed to scale and implement)\, a budget document for t
 he USD 20\,000\, a timeline with concrete deliverables inside 6 months\, a
 nd short bios for all team members. Award notifications mid-October 2026. 
 Questions to kylan@civichealthproject.org\; informal and exploratory conve
 rsations welcome.\n\nOrganisation: Normsy (a project of Civic Health Proje
 ct)\nLocation: Remote. Open to individuals and teams from academic\, acade
 mic-adjacent and industry backgrounds\; no stated country restriction. Res
 earch context is X/Twitter (proposals may be multi-platform but must inclu
 de X).\nAward: USD 1\,000 awarded to each of the top 5 proposals\, plus up
  to USD 20\,000 to fund R&D and/or testing with one selected partner indiv
 idual or team. Preference for budgets where at least 50 percent goes to re
 search costs (survey respondents\, LLM or X API credits). No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Proposals must set out concrete deliverables within 6 month
 s of starting\; the partnership is framed as the start of a potentially lo
 nger-term collaboration\n\nMore: https://normsy.ai/labs/openrfp?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://normsy.ai/labs/openrfp?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. Open to individuals and teams from academic\, academic-adj
 acent and industry backgrounds\; no stated country restriction. Research c
 ontext is X/Twitter (proposals may be multi-platform but must include X).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
