BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:internet-society-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261218
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261219
SUMMARY:Deadline: Internet Society Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Internet Society Fellowship supports people advancing Inter
 net connectivity\, policy or security in their communities. Fellows design
  and implement a practical project with deliverables such as policy briefs
 \, research reports\, training programs\, community engagement initiatives
 \, workshops or technical resources\, supported by a stipend of up to USD 
 5\,000\, a dedicated mentor\, weekly virtual training sessions and funded 
 travel to a global or regional Internet event. IMPORTANT PREREQUISITE: app
 licants must first complete the Pre-Fellowship Training modules\; enrollme
 nt for the training runs 23 September to 22 November 2026\, and eligible p
 articipants receive the Fellowship application link by email within 24 hou
 rs of completing it. ELIGIBILITY: open worldwide\; requires a high level o
 f English reading\, speaking and writing\, continuous Internet access thro
 ughout the program\, a valid passport (six months beyond anticipated trave
 l)\, at least four hours per week over 10 months\, and not currently parti
 cipating in a 2026 Internet Society fellowship. TIMELINE: applications rev
 iewed on a rolling basis and close 18 December 2026\; notifications 19 Feb
 ruary 2027\; program kick-off 22 February 2027\; project execution 1 June 
 to 30 November 2027\; graduation early December 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Int
 ernet Society\nLocation: Remote\, worldwide\, with funded travel to one gl
 obal or regional Internet event. Requires continuous Internet access\, str
 ong English\, and a passport valid at least six months beyond the anticipa
 ted travel.\nAward: Project stipend of up to USD 5\,000 (upon submission a
 nd approval of the project budget)\, plus travel support to attend a globa
 l or regional Internet event and a dedicated mentor aligned with the proje
 ct area. No application fee.\nDuration: 10 months (kick-off 22 February 20
 27\, graduation early December 2027)\, including a six-month mentored proj
 ect execution period from 1 June to 30 November 2027. Minimum commitment o
 f four hours per week.\n\nMore: https://www.internetsociety.org/fellowship
 /?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.internetsociety.org/fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, worldwide\, with funded travel to one global or regional 
 Internet event. Requires continuous Internet access\, strong English\, and
  a passport valid at least six months beyond the anticipated travel.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mit-solve-global-challenges-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261102
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261103
SUMMARY:Deadline: MIT Solve: 2027 Global Challenges
DESCRIPTION:MIT Solve's annual open innovation call selects tech-based solu
 tions to global challenges and backs them with funding and a nine-month su
 pport program. The 2027 cycle runs five parallel challenges on a shared ap
 plication deadline: the Global Climate Challenge\, Global Health Challenge
 \, Global Learning Challenge\, Global Economic Prosperity Challenge\, and 
 the Indigenous Communities Fellowship. WHAT SOLVERS GET: a USD 10\,000 gra
 nt funded by Solve for every selected team\; access to a pool of over USD 
 1 million in sponsor prize funding at selection\; tailored capacity-buildi
 ng workshops (marketing\, PR\, monitoring and evaluation\, business model 
 and more)\; leadership coaching\; networking with the MIT and Solve commun
 ity\; pro bono resources such as software and legal services\; event atten
 dance and media exposure\; and permanent Solver status. ELIGIBILITY: open 
 to applicants of all backgrounds worldwide with a technology-based solutio
 n addressing one of the challenge areas\; teams and organizations at vario
 us stages can apply. SELECTION: judged on alignment\, impact potential\, f
 easibility\, innovation\, human-centered design\, scalability\, partnershi
 p potential and technical feasibility. DEADLINE: 2 November 2026 at 12:00 
 PM EST\, shared across all 2027 challenges. Apply via the challenge pages 
 on solve.mit.edu.\n\nOrganisation: MIT Solve (Massachusetts Institute of T
 echnology)\nLocation: Remote\, worldwide. Nine-month virtual support progr
 am with event attendance and media exposure opportunities.\nAward: Every s
 elected Solver team receives a USD 10\,000 grant funded by Solve\, plus ac
 cess to a pool of over USD 1 million in prize funding at selection and add
 itional funding opportunities throughout the program. Sponsor prizes range
  from USD 100\,000 to 200\,000\, including the AI for Humanity Prize (up t
 o USD 150\,000\, Patrick J. McGovern Foundation)\, the Collegium Prize for
  Innovation (USD 100\,000)\, the Citizens Workforce Innovation Prize (USD 
 100\,000)\, the Crescent Enterprises AI for Social Innovation Prize (USD 1
 00\,000) and the Seeding the Future Prize (USD 150\,000). No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Nine-month support program\, plus permanent Solver status w
 ith ongoing access to future opportunities.\n\nMore: https://solve.mit.edu
 /challenges?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://solve.mit.edu/challenges?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, worldwide. Nine-month virtual support program with event 
 attendance and media exposure opportunities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nlnet-open-call-november-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261103
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261104
SUMMARY:Deadline: NLnet Foundation: Open Call November 2026 (Restack\, Code
 Supply and general fund)
DESCRIPTION:NLnet's current open call funds open-source technical developme
 nt: software and hardware released under free licenses. ROUTES: the Restac
 k fund (open internet stack)\, the CodeSupply fund\, and the general open 
 call for everything else\; accessibility and assistive-tech FOSS (screen r
 eaders\, a11y tooling\, accessible interfaces) was repeatedly funded under
  the predecessor NGI Zero programme and fits via the general route\, thoug
 h Restack's own scope text does not name accessibility\, so confirm scope 
 with NLnet before applying. NLnet also provides grantee support services i
 ncluding accessibility audits. ELIGIBILITY: individuals\, informal collect
 ives\, nonprofits\, SMEs and universities from any country\; no disability
  identification required. NOTE: proposals and projects generated with AI/L
 LMs are ineligible. APPLICATION: short online form at nlnet.nl/propose\; d
 ecisions within weeks\; calls recur roughly every two months\, so a missed
  deadline rolls to the next cycle. DEADLINE: 3 November 2026\, 12:00 CET (
 noon).\n\nOrganisation: Stichting NLnet (with European Commission backing\
 ; successor to the NGI Zero programme)\nLocation: Remote / worldwide. Indi
 viduals\, informal collectives\, nonprofits\, SMEs and universities from a
 ny country\; EU-backed funds want a European dimension\, and applicants ou
 tside Horizon Europe countries must explain the fit.\nAward: EUR 5\,000 to
  50\,000 for a first grant\, scalable in follow-ups\; the Restack fund alo
 ne will award EUR 7 million through 2030. No application fee.\nDuration: P
 roject-based\, typically up to about 12 months per plan\; calls recur roug
 hly every two months.\n\nMore: https://nlnet.nl/propose/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://nlnet.nl/propose/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote / worldwide. Individuals\, informal collectives\, nonprofit
 s\, SMEs and universities from any country\; EU-backed funds want a Europe
 an dimension\, and applicants outside Horizon Europe countries must explai
 n the fit.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mensch-maschine-visit-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mensch Maschine VISIT Residency Fellowship 2027: Energy G
 hosts (E-WERK Luckenwalde / Akademie der Künste)
DESCRIPTION:The Mensch Maschine VISIT Residency Program is an annual fellow
 ship offering four fully funded fellowships to artists from around the wor
 ld\, run by the JUNGE AKADEMIE of the Akademie der Künste (Berlin) with V
 ISIT\, the artist-in-residence programme of the E.ON Foundation\, E-WERK L
 uckenwalde and Ujazdowski Castle Centre for Contemporary Art (Warsaw). The
  2027 edition particularly encourages projects at the intersection of art\
 , energy and ecology exploring the theme ENERGY GHOSTS: the unseen dimensi
 ons of energy approached through mythology\, cosmology or speculative fict
 ion\, the invisible infrastructures\, histories and residues that haunt co
 ntemporary life\, and energy as an ecological\, political or spiritual for
 ce. Proposals are welcome from visual art\, performance\, film\, installat
 ion\, sound\, writing and new media. WHAT YOU GET: EUR 20\,000 per fellow 
 for artist fees and production costs of a new project (research and develo
 pment\, travel\, material and presentation costs)\; three fellows receive 
 studio space and accommodation at E-WERK Luckenwalde (a former power stati
 on about 40 minutes south of Berlin)\, one is accommodated by the Akademie
  der Künste in Berlin. The three-month residency runs roughly September t
 o November 2027 with on-site presence of at least 75% required\, a retreat
  at Ujazdowski Castle in Warsaw in September 2027\, events across the thre
 e venues\, and a closing symposium in October 2027 at the Akademie der Kü
 nste where fellows show work in progress alongside the 2026 cohort. JURY: 
 Nolan Oswald Dennis\; Chus Martinez (Institute Art Gender Nature\, TBA21)\
 ; Sarah Johanna Theurer (Fondation Beyeler)\; Anh-Linh Ngo (Akademie der K
 ünste)\; and Tiara Roxanne. APPLICATION: via the VISIT portal at visit-en
 ergy.com/bewerben. DEADLINE: 1 December 2026\, 23:59 CET.\n\nOrganisation:
  JUNGE AKADEMIE of the Akademie der Künste\, VISIT (E.ON Foundation)\, E-
 WERK Luckenwalde and Ujazdowski Castle Centre for Contemporary Art\nLocati
 on: E-WERK Luckenwalde\, Brandenburg\, Germany (3 fellows) and Akademie de
 r Künste\, Berlin (1 fellow)\, with a retreat at Ujazdowski Castle CCA in
  Warsaw\, Poland. Open to artists worldwide.\nAward: Four fully funded fel
 lowships of EUR 20\,000 each\, intended for artist fees and production cos
 ts of a new project (research and development\, travel\, materials and pre
 sentation costs)\, plus studio space and accommodation for the three-month
  residency.\nDuration: Three months\, roughly September to November 2027 (
 the AdK notice gives mid-August to mid-November)\; fellows must live and w
 ork on site at least 75% of the time.\n\nMore: https://adk.de/news/2026/10
 /open-call-mensch-maschine-stipendium?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://adk.de/news/2026/10/open-call-mensch-maschine-stipendium?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:E-WERK Luckenwalde\, Brandenburg\, Germany (3 fellows) and Akademi
 e der Künste\, Berlin (1 fellow)\, with a retreat at Ujazdowski Castle CC
 A in Warsaw\, Poland. Open to artists worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bellingcat-ai-engineer-agentic-contractor-2026@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261108
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261109
SUMMARY:Deadline: Bellingcat Contractor: AI Engineer (Open Source Agentic S
 ystems)
DESCRIPTION:Bellingcat\, the open source investigations collective\, is hir
 ing a contractor AI engineer to build agentic systems that support its inv
 estigators. (Listed on Grant Desk as an exception to the no-jobs rule: a r
 emote\, paid\, public-interest AI contract.) THE ROLE: build\, test\, depl
 oy and monitor multi-agent workflows for open source investigations\; deve
 lop agentic pipelines with human oversight\, feedback loops and evaluation
  metrics\; architect and maintain cloud infrastructure for hosting LLM har
 nesses and monitoring agents using token-as-a-service providers\; develop 
 MCP servers and data bridges connecting agents to open source research too
 ls and databases\; and create evaluation frameworks for the performance an
 d reasoning of agentic pipelines. WHAT YOU GET: an annual fee of USD 80\,0
 00 to 93\,000 for full-time work (part-time 20 hours/week also possible)\,
  on an initial 12-month contract\, working remotely with some flexibility 
 to work from other countries for a limited number of weeks per year. ELIGI
 BILITY: hands-on full-stack development experience including infrastructur
 e maintenance and server-side data workflows\; experience with agentic AI 
 workflows and open-source harnesses\; experience with human-in-the-loop de
 sign (approval checks\, feedback loops)\; a collaborative record of workin
 g with non-technical colleagues on public interest technology\; English pr
 oficiency\; and a time zone overlapping with Europe. Experience with missi
 on-driven organisations or open source / public interest tech is a plus. A
 PPLICATION: via Google Form\; Bellingcat asks that applications not be AI-
 generated (AI for grammar checks is fine). Review is rolling from 21 Octob
 er and interviews may start before the close. DEADLINE: 8 November 2026.\n
 \nOrganisation: Bellingcat\nLocation: Remote. Requires a time zone with su
 fficient overlap with Bellingcat's Europe-based team.\nAward: Annual contr
 actor fee of USD 80\,000 to 93\,000 for full-time (40 hours/week)\, depend
 ing on experience and track record\; a part-time (20 hours/week) option is
  also offered\, rate not stated.\nDuration: Initial 12-month contract\, fu
 ll-time or part-time\, starting as soon as possible.\n\nMore: https://www.
 bellingcat.com/contractor-ai-engineer-open-source-agentic-systems/?utm_sou
 rce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.bellingcat.com/contractor-ai-engineer-open-source-agentic-s
 ystems/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. Requires a time zone with sufficient overlap with Bellingc
 at's Europe-based team.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jsk-journalism-fellowship-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261202
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261203
SUMMARY:Deadline: JSK Journalism Fellowship 2027-28 (John S. Knight Fellows
 hips at Stanford)
DESCRIPTION:Stanford's nine-month residential fellowship for journalists an
 d people who work in service of journalism (editors\, producers\, photogra
 phers\, audience and community builders\, publishers\, operations and stra
 tegy staff). Fellows bring a question about journalism they cannot let go 
 of rather than a fixed project\, and pursue it with a small international 
 cohort\, individual coaching\, customised workshops and the run of Stanfor
 d\; many fellows work on AI\, platforms\, information ecosystems and audie
 nce trust. ELIGIBILITY: at least five years of professional experience (st
 udent work and internships don't count)\; no degree or newsroom background
  required\; freelancers must do most of their work in journalism. Not elig
 ible: PR or communications staff\, government employees\, full-time academ
 ics\, staff of non-editorially-independent media\, degree seekers\, book o
 r reporting projects. APPLICATION: via SurveyMonkey Apply (jsk.smapply.org
 )\; deadline 2 December 2026 at 1pm Pacific\, no extensions. Info webinars
  on 21 October and 5\, 6 and 12 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: John S. Kn
 ight Journalism Fellowships at Stanford\nLocation: Stanford\, California\,
  USA (residential 9-month fellowship). Citizens of countries where the US 
 has suspended J visa issuance cannot be offered a fellowship unless they a
 pply with another unrestricted citizenship.\nAward: USD 135\,000 stipend f
 or nine months\, plus Stanford tuition and health insurance and a health s
 upplement for dependents on Stanford coverage. No application fee.\nDurati
 on: 9 months in residence at Stanford\, September 2027 to May 2028\n\nMore
 : https://jsk.stanford.edu/become-a-fellow?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://jsk.stanford.edu/become-a-fellow?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Stanford\, California\, USA (residential 9-month fellowship). Citi
 zens of countries where the US has suspended J visa issuance cannot be off
 ered a fellowship unless they apply with another unrestricted citizenship.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mozilla-incubator-human-scale-ai-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261120
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261121
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mozilla Foundation Incubator: Human-Scale AI cohort 2027 
 (call for proposals)
DESCRIPTION:The Mozilla Foundation Incubator's next cohort funds seven Huma
 n-Scale AI projects: working AI technologies that are small (small models\
 , less compute\, able to run locally or on low-power devices)\, intentiona
 l (users and communities get insight into and control over their data\, an
 d systems can be configured\, forked or community-governed) and tailored (
 fit-for-purpose for a specific community\, context or problem\, with parti
 cular interest in resource-constrained regions\, people with disabilities 
 and other underserved groups). Projects must already be deployed with the 
 people they serve\, even at pilot scale\, and show early product-community
  fit. Not wanted: concepts without working technology\, standalone governa
 nce or policy work\, generic chatbots\, or products whose core depends on 
 a frontier model or a slimmed-down version of one. The cohort runs for 12 
 months from April 2027 with coaching\, a Mozilla programme officer\, netwo
 rk access and a showcase at the Mozilla Festival\; required sessions are a
 bout one or two hour-long virtual meetings a month\, and teams must work i
 n the open. ELIGIBILITY: worldwide\; you do not need to be a nonprofit but
  the project must have a charitable purpose and you must be legally able t
 o receive Mozilla Foundation grant funds. Applications in English (transla
 tion tools welcome). Multi-stage process: an initial project proposal\, th
 en a full application for a shortlist. Info session 12 November 2026\, 17:
 00 UTC. DEADLINE: Friday 20 November 2026\, 11:59PM PST (UTC-8).\n\nOrgani
 sation: Mozilla Foundation (Mozilla Foundation Incubator)\nLocation: Remot
 e cohort worldwide\, with a platform at the Mozilla Festival.\nAward: USD 
 300\,000 non-dilutive funding plus 12 months of support for each of seven 
 selected projects\; projects showing strong progress may receive up to USD
  200\,000 more and two further years of support\, for a total of up to USD
  500\,000 over three years. No application fee.\nDuration: 12 months from 
 April 2027\, with possible follow-on support for two more years.\n\nMore: 
 https://www.mozillafoundation.org/en/what-we-do/grantmaking/incubator/huma
 n-scale-ai/call-for-proposals/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.mozillafoundation.org/en/what-we-do/grantmaking/incubator/h
 uman-scale-ai/call-for-proposals/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote cohort worldwide\, with a platform at the Mozilla Festival.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:foresight-ai-science-safety-nodes-rfp-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: Foresight Institute: AI for Science and Safety Nodes RFP 
 2026 (Local Compute\; Coordination and Accountability\; AI-first Science)
DESCRIPTION:Foresight Institute's AI for Science and Safety Nodes request f
 or proposals funds open\, AI-first projects across three calls: I. Local C
 ompute (compute that individuals and communities physically own and contro
 l)\, II. Coordination and Accountability (supercollaboration and decentral
 ised alignment\, human empowerment\, and AI insurance and open governance:
  multi-agent oversight and verification\, tools for collective deliberatio
 n\, decision rules for when human control adds value\, and independent ris
 k assessment or insurance mechanisms for frontier labs)\, and III. AI-firs
 t Science (automated nanotechnology\, whole-brain emulation\, frontier bio
 ). Grantees can also get workspace in the San Francisco or Berlin AI Nodes
  (a community xNode runs in Cape Town) and private compute. All funded cod
 e\, data and outputs must be open-sourced. Projects are judged on fit with
  the focus area\, impact on reducing existential risk from AI\, feasibilit
 y within 1 to 3 years\, AI-first working methods\, ability to execute\, an
 d high-risk\, high-reward potential. ELIGIBILITY: individuals\, teams and 
 organisations worldwide\; nonprofits and for-profits welcome (for-profits 
 must justify the need for grant funding). Priority goes to applicants who 
 want to work in person at one of the Nodes\, but it is not required. Revie
 w takes about three months\; applications can opt in to sharing with Light
 cone Commons. DEADLINE: 31 October 2026\, 23:59 PDT.\n\nOrganisation: Fore
 sight Institute\nLocation: Remote-friendly worldwide\; optional in-person 
 membership at the Foresight AI Nodes in San Francisco or Berlin.\nAward: G
 rants typically USD 30\,000 to 100\,000 (smaller awards for the Human Empo
 werment and AI Insurance and Open Governance areas\, larger for the rest\;
  bigger amounts possible for the right project)\, usually paid as one lump
  sum\, with up to 10 percent overhead. Optional office space in San Franci
 sco or Berlin and access to private compute clusters. No application fee.\
 nDuration: Short-term and multi-year projects\; larger multi-year grants a
 re paid in milestone tranches.\n\nMore: https://foresight.org/grants/ai-sc
 ience-safety-nodes-rfp/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://foresight.org/grants/ai-science-safety-nodes-rfp/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote-friendly worldwide\; optional in-person membership at the F
 oresight AI Nodes in San Francisco or Berlin.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:rji-professional-innovation-fellowships-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261024T212627Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270108
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270109
SUMMARY:Deadline: RJI Professional Innovation Fellowships 2027 (Individual\
 , Institutional and Emerging Technology tracks)
DESCRIPTION:The Reynolds Journalism Institute at the University of Missouri
  funds people and teams to build and launch a tool\, workflow or product t
 hat helps newsrooms\, in three tracks: Individual (USD 75\,000)\, Institut
 ional (USD 75\,000\, paid to a host organisation or the fellow) and Emergi
 ng Technology (USD 100\,000\, where AI or another new technology must be e
 ssential to the solution). The output must be a free\, open-source resourc
 e usable by US journalists\; tools limited to one city or state are unlike
 ly to be funded. The fellowship is remote and runs for eight months from 2
 8 June 2027 with an in-person kickoff at RJI and a couple of paid visits. 
 ELIGIBILITY: anyone\, anywhere\; no age\, background or journalism credent
 ial required. Applications open 2 November 2026 on Submittable\; a shortli
 st is invited in early to mid February 2027 to submit a fuller application
 \, with finalist interviews 5 to 23 April 2027. DEADLINE: 8 January 2027 (
 first round\; no time zone stated\, RJI is on US Central time).\n\nOrganis
 ation: Reynolds Journalism Institute (University of Missouri)\nLocation: R
 emote\, with a few paid visits to the Reynolds Journalism Institute in Col
 umbia\, Missouri\, United States.\nAward: USD 75\,000 for the Individual a
 nd Institutional tracks\, USD 100\,000 for the Emerging Technology track\,
  paid in three instalments tied to deliverables\; RJI also pays for the fe
 llow's visits to its Columbia\, Missouri campus. No application fee.\nDura
 tion: Eight months from 28 June 2027.\n\nMore: https://rjionline.org/about
 -rji-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://rjionline.org/about-rji-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with a few paid visits to the Reynolds Journalism Institu
 te in Columbia\, Missouri\, United States.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:zoud-gamelab-finlit-game-jam-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
